            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "active_joints reduces a full 37-row Shadow pose to the 20 joints most work uses.\n\nWHY 20:\nThe shadow node sends 37 rows per body. Twenty of them are the joints that carry the movement of the body: the spine and head, the hips, knees and ankles, the shoulder blades, shoulders, elbows and wrists. Reducing early keeps everything downstream smaller and in one agreed order.\n\nWHAT IT TAKES:\nA pose of 37 rows, as a numpy array or a torch tensor - straight from the shadow node's 'body N quaternions' outlet, from a take, or from shadow_pose.\nAnything else produces nothing: a pose that is already 20 rows, a flat array of 148 numbers, or a list.\n\nWHAT IT SENDS:\nThe 20 rows, in this order: base of skull, upper vertebrae, mid vertebrae, lower vertebrae, spine pelvis, pelvis anchor, left hip, knee and ankle, right hip, knee and ankle, left shoulder blade, shoulder, elbow and wrist, then the same four on the right.\n\nIt picks rows and does not change them, and sends the same kind it received:\nnumpy in, numpy out; torch in, torch out.\n\nSYNTAX:\nactive_joints\n\nEXAMPLE:\nactive_joints\n\nINPUTS:\n\nfull pose quats in:\nA 37-row Shadow pose.\n\nOUTPUT:\n\nactive joint quats out:\nThe 20-row pose.\n\nRELATED:\nshadow_pose builds a 37-row pose from single joints, as in the example. active_pose shows a 20-joint pose with its joint names. body_to_joints splits a pose into one outlet per joint.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 720,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "active_joints reduces a full 37-row Shadow pose to the 20 joints most work uses.\n\nWHY 20:\nThe shadow node sends 37 rows per body. Twenty of them are the joints that carry the movement of the body: the spine and head, the hips, knees and ankles, the shoulder blades, shoulders, elbows and wrists. Reducing early keeps everything downstream smaller and in one agreed order.\n\nWHAT IT TAKES:\nA pose of 37 rows, as a numpy array or a torch tensor - straight from the shadow node's 'body N quaternions' outlet, from a take, or from shadow_pose.\nAnything else produces nothing: a pose that is already 20 rows, a flat array of 148 numbers, or a list.\n\nWHAT IT SENDS:\nThe 20 rows, in this order: base of skull, upper vertebrae, mid vertebrae, lower vertebrae, spine pelvis, pelvis anchor, left hip, knee and ankle, right hip, knee and ankle, left shoulder blade, shoulder, elbow and wrist, then the same four on the right.\n\nIt picks rows and does not change them, and sends the same kind it received:\nnumpy in, numpy out; torch in, torch out.\n\nSYNTAX:\nactive_joints\n\nEXAMPLE:\nactive_joints\n\nINPUTS:\n\nfull pose quats in:\nA 37-row Shadow pose.\n\nOUTPUT:\n\nactive joint quats out:\nThe 20-row pose.\n\nRELATED:\nshadow_pose builds a 37-row pose from single joints, as in the example. active_pose shows a 20-joint pose with its joint names. body_to_joints splits a pose into one outlet per joint."
        },
